                <pb facs="0282" n="187" xml:id="pg187"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/13509: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Canton</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Spiker</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Canton</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-06-01T16:00:00+08:00">June 1, 1936—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 1—2:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Following supplements paragraph 1 of my June 1, 10 a.m. There are persistent
                    local reports that Japanese machinations using Kwangsi leaders as dupes or
                    willing tools against Chiang resulted in the document alleged to be Hu’s will
                    which document lends support to Japanese so-called anti-Communistic activities
                    in North China and at the same time denounces Chiang as a dictator: the
                    anti-Japanese item in the document serving as a screen. This telegram has been
                    sent to the Department and the Embassy.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Spiker</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
